review_text_for_llm:
Great game, just some fun boomer shootering romp. Tons of different enemy types and weapons, lots of varied environments, lots of inventive setpieces in each level, despite the (seeming?) limitations, great level design. Level editor! Good stuff all around.

If I were to talk about the cons (I personally have played nearly every boomer shooter under the sun, new and old, so this may be biased) 
- I'd say it's a bit too easy. I beat it on nightmare, and never really had any trouble, however I feel like this could easily be solved just messing with enemy stats. The enemy compositions are fine for the most part, they just feel a bit weak of health and slow to react. I felt like the last few levels were getting to a good place in terms of difficulty, but then the game ended. The next criticism ties into this;
- The game floods you with supplies at all times, so resource management is close to non-existent (user maps can sidestep this entirely). No naked starts, you bring your entire arsenal throughout all levels, all 500 kilo-tons of it;
- The inventory items go a bit overboard. I only used like 2 of the items throughout (health potion and rapid-fire scroll) and there are around 10 of

I recently found my old gameboy colour and sadly my copy of Colin McRae Rally has stopped working. I found myself wishing for a modern version, and without even trying to find it my prayers were answered when this popped up on the steam homepage.

Genuinely so much fun, although normal difficulty feels a bit like your car is always overpowered in races. Easy to pick up, playing on keyboard and the cars feel heavy enough to be slightly harder but not so much that it feels sluggish.

@ViJuDa where can we make feature requests?

WOW! This little game has been such a surprising boost to my productivity. I've struggled a lot with keeping focused, but ChillPulse offers a fantastic spin on the Pomodoro technique and I'm completely hooked.

The game is both simplistic and chock full of features:

✿ 𝓦𝓸𝓻𝓴 ✿
• Adjustable Pomodoro timer (default is 25 minutes focused work, 5 minutes chill time);
• To-do list;
• Journal with a calendar view, allowing you to see what tasks have been completed each day and the total amount of minutes spent in focused work;

✿ 𝓕𝓾𝓷 ✿
• Lots of cute pets;
• Little coffee game where you can find different recipes using various combinations of three ingredients
• Awesome music: lo-fi, jazz, etc.;
• Ambience: configure your perfect blend of wind, crackling fire, birdsong, etc.;
• Several background locations to chill in;

Having a little friend on my second monitor is very comforting. Every time I need to quickly recharge my brain during focus time I look at the game and pat my pet duck.

I recommend this game with all my heart. I simply love this series where you need to find over 100 cats in a black-n-white labyrinthine world. First the streets, then the amusement park and now we have breeze village. Although the number of cats have been reduced, it still gives you the old vibe characteristic of these series. If you even get stuck and don't want to hit the hint button, follow the meow.

PROS:
- Black-n-white world with intricate design;
- Over 100 cats to find and other items;
- Relaxing joyful music;
- Easy to learn mechanics;
- Easy to medium to obtain achievements.

NEUTER:
- Bigger world and more cats!

CONS:
- Nothing else.

Next time I want a bigger world and more cats just like in the Amusement Park game.

Selewi is perhaps the only developer in the world that can seriously compete with PuppetCombo and TortureStar when it comes to VHS-style retro slasher games. With his third game, he has officially established his own trademark, that consists in a very clever fusion between the 80's slasher theme and the ResidentEvil play style. Fixed camera, tank controls, low poly but extremely curated graphics, film grane, tons of puzzles to solve, and of course a deranged sicko on our trail! The admixture of all these elements works flawlessly well, and delivers an extremely fun, yet utterly terrifying experience. In particular, there are two elements of Selewi's games that I personally love:
- the puzzles are maybe simple, but very fun to solve, they keep you constantly entertained, but never needlessly stuck, they never interrupt the flow of the game; overall I find this a perfect balance;
- the killer does not blindly patrol the scene like a moron who has nothing better to do, but instead he suddenly ambushes you when you least expect it; the result: you get surprised with little to no forewarning, you can never relax, you must be ready to react at any moment, because the freak could come out

Play It ; It Will Give You What You Want ;
An Experience Like ; No Other ;
As Light And Dark Converge ;
Infinitely Twisting ; Among Stars ;
To Shatter ; The Tyranny Of Fate ;

(Please offer the soundtrack for purchase as well!)

[b] A true gem for those who love labyrinths and mysteries.[/b] 
This game offers:
- a vast, slowly shifting labyrinthine gigastructure;
- surreal works of art scattered throughout;
- cryptic secrets about the labyrinth and its strange inhabitants;
- multiple endings—more than three—shaped by your curiosity and choices;
- a haunting, enigmatic atmosphere that keeps you on edge.
- if you look carefully, you will find secrets, shortcuts and hidden ways!

The excerpts from The Labyrinth Chronicles that appear during loading screens were a standout touch. [spoiler]The book doesn’t actually exist, yet it’s filled with clever nods to real-world authors and literary works.[/spoiler] The whole experience gave me strong House of Leaves vibes by Mark Z. Danielewski—both in tone and structure.

The mood within the labyrinth swings between moments of breathtaking beauty and unsettling eeriness. You're clearly not alone, but the place still feels abandoned and isolating. That’s what makes the distant growls echoing from behind the walls, or the sudden appearance of a shadowy figure—just a statue, thankfully—even more disturbing.

[b]Minuses[/b] :
